// CLF-C02 flashcards. One object per card: d = domain (1 to 4), t = topic, q = question, a = answer.
// Source: the notes/ files in this repo. Edit here, commit, and the page picks it up.
window.CARDS = [

// Domain 1: Cloud Concepts (24%)
{d:1,t:"Six advantages",q:"What are the six advantages of cloud computing?",a:"Trade fixed expense for variable expense. Benefit from massive economies of scale. Stop guessing capacity. Increase speed and agility. Stop spending money running and maintaining data centres. Go global in minutes."},
{d:1,t:"Six advantages",q:"A company pays only for what it uses instead of buying servers up front. Which advantage is that?",a:"Trade fixed expense for variable expense."},
{d:1,t:"Six advantages",q:"Why can AWS charge less per unit than a company running its own servers?",a:"Economies of scale. AWS buys at huge volume and passes the savings on."},
{d:1,t:"Six advantages",q:"A team no longer has to guess how many servers to buy for next year. Which advantage is that?",a:"Stop guessing capacity."},
{d:1,t:"Six advantages",q:"A team can get new servers in minutes and try ideas faster. Which advantage is that?",a:"Increase speed and agility."},
{d:1,t:"Six advantages",q:"A company closes its data centre and spends the time on its product instead. Which advantage is that?",a:"Stop spending money running and maintaining data centres."},
{d:1,t:"Six advantages",q:"An app is deployed to users on three continents in an afternoon. Which advantage is that?",a:"Go global in minutes."},
{d:1,t:"Terms",q:"What is the difference between scalability and elasticity?",a:"Scalability means capacity can grow (bigger instances or more of them). Elasticity means capacity grows and shrinks automatically with demand."},
{d:1,t:"Terms",q:"What does agility mean in cloud terms?",a:"How fast you can provision resources and experiment. Minutes instead of weeks."},
{d:1,t:"Terms",q:"What is the difference between high availability and fault tolerance?",a:"High availability keeps the service running when a component fails, usually with a short blip. Fault tolerance means zero interruption at all. Fault tolerance is stricter and costs more."},
{d:1,t:"Terms",q:"What is disaster recovery for, and how is it measured?",a:"Recovering after a Region-wide or site-wide event. Measured by RTO and RPO. DR is cross-Region; high availability is cross-AZ."},
{d:1,t:"Terms",q:"What does RTO measure?",a:"Recovery time objective: how long until the service is back up."},
{d:1,t:"Terms",q:"What does RPO measure?",a:"Recovery point objective: how much data you can afford to lose, as the time since the last good copy."},
{d:1,t:"Terms",q:"What do you get from running in multiple AZs, and what do you get from running in multiple Regions?",a:"Multiple AZs give high availability. Multiple Regions give disaster recovery and lower latency for distant users."},
{d:1,t:"Terms",q:"Which disaster recovery strategy is cheapest, and which is fastest?",a:"Backup and restore is cheapest and slowest. Multi-site active/active is fastest and most expensive. Pilot light and warm standby sit in between."},
{d:1,t:"Well-Architected",q:"What are the six Well-Architected pillars?",a:"Operational Excellence, Security, Reliability, Performance Efficiency, Cost Optimization, Sustainability."},
{d:1,t:"Well-Architected",q:"Which pillar covers automating deployments, infrastructure as code and learning from failures?",a:"Operational Excellence."},
{d:1,t:"Well-Architected",q:"Which pillar covers least privilege, traceability and encrypting data at rest and in transit?",a:"Security."},
{d:1,t:"Well-Architected",q:"Which pillar covers recovering automatically from an AZ failure and testing recovery?",a:"Reliability."},
{d:1,t:"Well-Architected",q:"Which pillar covers choosing the right instance type or going serverless?",a:"Performance Efficiency."},
{d:1,t:"Well-Architected",q:"Which pillar covers right-sizing and buying Reserved Instances?",a:"Cost Optimization."},
{d:1,t:"Well-Architected",q:"Which pillar covers reducing idle resources to lower your carbon footprint?",a:"Sustainability."},
{d:1,t:"Well-Architected",q:"What is the difference between the Well-Architected Tool and Trusted Advisor?",a:"The Well-Architected Tool is a free questionnaire you fill in by hand; it reviews one workload against the pillars. Trusted Advisor is automated and checks your account continuously."},
{d:1,t:"CAF",q:"What are the six Cloud Adoption Framework perspectives?",a:"Business side: Business, People, Governance. Technical side: Platform, Security, Operations."},
{d:1,t:"CAF",q:"What are the four Cloud Adoption Framework transformation phases?",a:"Envision, Align, Launch, Scale."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"What are the 7 Rs of migration?",a:"Rehost, Replatform, Repurchase, Refactor, Retire, Retain, Relocate."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"An application is moved to AWS with no changes at all. Which R is that, and which service does it?",a:"Rehost (lift and shift), using Application Migration Service (MGN)."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"An application is moved to AWS and its database is swapped to RDS on the way. Which R is that?",a:"Replatform (lift, tinker and shift)."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"A company replaces its in-house CRM with a SaaS product. Which R is that?",a:"Repurchase (drop and shop)."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"An application is rewritten as serverless microservices. Which R is that?",a:"Refactor, also called re-architect."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"During discovery a team finds an app nobody uses any more and switches it off. Which R is that?",a:"Retire."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"A system stays on-premises for now because of a compliance rule. Which R is that?",a:"Retain."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"VMware virtual machines are moved to VMware Cloud on AWS without any changes. Which R is that?",a:"Relocate."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"Which migration service is for databases, which for files, which for offline transfer and which for whole servers?",a:"DMS for databases. DataSync for files. The Snow family for offline transfer. MGN for servers."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"Which service gives you one place to track the progress of all your migrations?",a:"AWS Migration Hub."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"Which service inventories your on-premises servers and maps their dependencies before a migration?",a:"AWS Application Discovery Service."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"Which service migrates a database with minimal downtime?",a:"Database Migration Service (DMS). If the database engine changes too, add the Schema Conversion Tool (SCT)."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"Which service copies files from an on-premises NFS or SMB share to S3 over the network, on a schedule?",a:"AWS DataSync."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"Which service gives partners an SFTP endpoint that lands files in S3?",a:"AWS Transfer Family."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"A site has 500 TB to move and a slow internet link. What do you use?",a:"A physical Snow device, Snowball Edge. The link cannot carry it in time."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"What are the Snow family devices, smallest to largest?",a:"Snowcone (a few TB, no longer offered), Snowball Edge (up to about 80 TB per device, the current option), Snowmobile (an exabyte truck, retired in 2024)."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"When should you consider a Snow device instead of transferring online?",a:"When the online transfer would take more than about a week."},
{d:1,t:"Migration",q:"What is the difference between Migration Evaluator and the Migration Acceleration Program?",a:"Migration Evaluator builds the business case from your on-prem data. The Migration Acceleration Program (MAP) is AWS funding and help to actually migrate."},
{d:1,t:"Economics",q:"What is the difference between CapEx and OpEx?",a:"CapEx is capital expense: buying servers up front and depreciating them. OpEx is operating expense: paying monthly for what you use. Cloud turns CapEx into OpEx."},
{d:1,t:"Economics",q:"What should a total cost of ownership comparison include?",a:"Hardware, software, licences, facilities, power, cooling, real estate, staff and hardware refresh over the asset's lifetime, not just the server price."},
{d:1,t:"Economics",q:"Which tools recommend right-sizing an instance?",a:"Compute Optimizer and Cost Explorer."},
{d:1,t:"Economics",q:"A licence is tied to physical sockets or cores. Which EC2 option do you need?",a:"Dedicated Hosts. License Manager tracks the usage."},
{d:1,t:"Economics",q:"In what order does the exam expect you to cut cost?",a:"Turn it off, right-size, pick the right pricing model (RI, Savings Plan, Spot), use managed or serverless services, move to cheaper storage tiers."},
{d:1,t:"Economics",q:"An answer choice says it will reduce operational overhead. What kind of service is it usually pointing at?",a:"A managed service."},
{d:1,t:"Economics",q:"How do Reserved Instances and Savings Plans relate to pay as you go?",a:"Pay as you go is the default. Reserved Instances and Savings Plans are discounts on top of it in return for a commitment."},

// Domain 2: Security and Compliance (30%)
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"In the shared responsibility model, what is AWS responsible for and what is the customer responsible for?",a:"AWS: security OF the cloud. Hardware, facilities, hypervisor, global infrastructure, the software under managed services. Customer: security IN the cloud. Data, IAM, OS patching on EC2, network configuration, encryption choices, application code."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"Who patches the operating system of an EC2 instance?",a:"The customer."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"Who patches the operating system under an RDS instance?",a:"AWS. The same goes for Lambda."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"Who configures the security group on an RDS instance?",a:"The customer. Network configuration inside the VPC is always the customer's."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"What are the three shared controls?",a:"Patch management, configuration management, awareness and training. AWS does its side, the customer does theirs."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"What is the customer responsible for no matter how managed the service is?",a:"Their data, IAM, encryption decisions and network configuration inside the VPC."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"What is AWS responsible for no matter which service you use?",a:"Physical security, the hypervisor, edge locations and the hardware under managed services."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"Rank EC2, RDS, Fargate and Lambda from least to most AWS responsibility.",a:"EC2, then RDS, then Fargate, then Lambda. The more managed the service, the more AWS owns."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"For a Lambda function, what does the customer own?",a:"The function code, its IAM execution role, any secrets in environment variables, and its dependencies."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"For an S3 bucket, what does the customer own?",a:"The bucket policy, ACLs, Block Public Access settings, versioning and the choice of encryption."},
{d:2,t:"Shared responsibility",q:"Who is responsible for compliance, AWS or the customer?",a:"Both. AWS holds the infrastructure certifications. The customer is responsible for configuring and using the services in a compliant way."},
{d:2,t:"Compliance",q:"Where do you download AWS's SOC 2 report or PCI attestation?",a:"AWS Artifact. It holds AWS's own compliance reports and agreements such as the HIPAA BAA. It does not assess your compliance."},
{d:2,t:"Compliance",q:"Which service continuously collects evidence for your own audit against a framework?",a:"AWS Audit Manager."},
{d:2,t:"Compliance",q:"Which service aggregates security findings across accounts and scores them against standards like CIS?",a:"AWS Security Hub."},
{d:2,t:"Compliance",q:"Which service proves who did what, when and from where?",a:"CloudTrail."},
{d:2,t:"Compliance",q:"Which service proves how a resource was configured at a given point in time?",a:"AWS Config."},
{d:2,t:"Encryption",q:"How is data encrypted in transit on AWS?",a:"TLS over HTTPS endpoints, VPN tunnels, and certificates from ACM. AWS provides the HTTPS endpoints; the customer chooses to use them."},
{d:2,t:"Encryption",q:"How is data encrypted at rest on AWS?",a:"With KMS keys through options such as SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, SSE-C, EBS encryption and RDS encryption. The customer turns it on, AWS does the work."},
{d:2,t:"Encryption",q:"What is client-side encryption?",a:"The customer encrypts the data before uploading it. AWS never sees the plaintext or the key."},
{d:2,t:"Encryption",q:"What is the difference between KMS and CloudHSM?",a:"KMS is a multi-tenant managed service where AWS runs the hardware and you manage keys and policies; it integrates with nearly every service. CloudHSM is a single-tenant hardware appliance in your VPC where you manage everything. Both are FIPS 140-2 Level 3."},
{d:2,t:"Encryption",q:"A regulator requires keys to live in dedicated, single-tenant hardware that you control. Which service?",a:"CloudHSM."},
{d:2,t:"Encryption",q:"What are the three types of KMS key?",a:"AWS owned (you never see it), AWS managed (named like aws/s3, free, rotated yearly), customer managed (you create it, pay for it and set rotation)."},
{d:2,t:"Encryption",q:"How long is the waiting period before a customer managed KMS key is deleted?",a:"7 to 30 days."},
{d:2,t:"Logging",q:"What is the difference between CloudTrail, Config and CloudWatch?",a:"CloudTrail records API calls (who did what). Config records resource configuration over time (what state was it in). CloudWatch holds metrics, logs and alarms."},
{d:2,t:"Logging",q:"Which service tells you who deleted a bucket?",a:"CloudTrail."},
{d:2,t:"Logging",q:"Which service tells you whether a security group was ever open to the whole internet?",a:"AWS Config, with a managed rule."},
{d:2,t:"Logging",q:"Which service holds what your application printed to its logs at a given time?",a:"CloudWatch Logs."},
{d:2,t:"Logging",q:"What does CloudTrail record by default, and what is off by default?",a:"On by default: 90 days of management events viewable in the console. Off by default: a trail that delivers to S3, and data events such as S3 object reads or Lambda invokes."},
{d:2,t:"Governance",q:"What is least privilege?",a:"Granting only the permissions a task needs. It is the default answer to any question about how permissions should be set."},
{d:2,t:"Governance",q:"What does a service control policy do?",a:"It sets the maximum permissions available to the accounts in an OU. It does not grant anything on its own, and it does not apply to the management account."},
{d:2,t:"Governance",q:"An IAM user has an allow on all S3 actions, but the account's SCP denies deleting buckets. Can the user delete a bucket?",a:"No. Effective permissions are the intersection, and an SCP deny cannot be overridden by an IAM allow."},
{d:2,t:"Governance",q:"What is Control Tower?",a:"A prebuilt landing zone on top of Organizations. It creates accounts from a factory and applies guardrails: preventive ones as SCPs, detective ones as Config rules."},
{d:2,t:"Governance",q:"In what order should you weigh the factors when choosing a Region?",a:"Compliance and data sovereignty first, then latency to users, then whether the services you need are available there, then price."},
{d:2,t:"Governance",q:"Does AWS copy your data to another Region on its own?",a:"No. Data stays in the Region you put it in unless you set up replication."},
{d:2,t:"Governance",q:"Apart from SCPs, what does AWS Organizations give you?",a:"Consolidated billing, volume discount tiers pooled across accounts, Reserved Instance and Savings Plan discounts shared across accounts, and organisational units."},
{d:2,t:"Root user",q:"Which actions can only the root user perform?",a:"Change the account email or root password, change the support plan, close the account, restore IAM user permissions after a lockout, enable MFA Delete on an S3 bucket, register as a seller in the RI Marketplace, sign up for GovCloud, and activate IAM access to billing."},
{d:2,t:"Root user",q:"How should the root user be handled?",a:"Turn on MFA, delete its access keys, lock the credentials away, and never use it for day-to-day work."},
{d:2,t:"Root user",q:"Which MFA types does IAM support, and which one does it not?",a:"Supported: virtual MFA apps, FIDO security keys and passkeys, hardware TOTP tokens. Not supported: SMS."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What is an IAM user?",a:"A long-term identity with a password or access keys, for one specific person or a legacy application that cannot use a role."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What is an IAM group?",a:"A collection of users that policies attach to, for managing people by job function. Groups cannot be nested and cannot be named as the principal in a policy."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What is an IAM role?",a:"An identity with no long-term credentials. It is assumed to get temporary credentials, by an EC2 instance, a Lambda function, another account or a federated user."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What is an IAM policy?",a:"A JSON document of Effect, Action, Resource and Condition. It is the only thing that actually grants or denies permissions."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What is the difference between an identity-based policy and a resource-based policy?",a:"An identity-based policy attaches to a user, group or role, and the principal is implied. A resource-based policy attaches to the resource itself, such as a bucket or queue, and must name the principal."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What is a permissions boundary?",a:"A cap on the maximum permissions an identity-based policy can grant to a user or role. It grants nothing by itself."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What is the difference between AWS managed, customer managed and inline policies?",a:"AWS managed: maintained by AWS, reusable everywhere. Customer managed: written by you, reusable within your account, for exact least privilege. Inline: embedded in one identity and deleted with it."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"How is an IAM request evaluated?",a:"The default is deny. An explicit deny anywhere wins over any allow. Otherwise there must be an allow in an identity or resource policy. SCPs and permissions boundaries must also allow it, but they never grant on their own."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"When would you use IAM, when IAM Identity Center, and when Cognito?",a:"IAM for permissions inside one account. IAM Identity Center for employees signing in once to many accounts and SaaS apps. Cognito for the customers of your own app."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What is the difference between a Cognito user pool and an identity pool?",a:"A user pool handles authentication: a directory of app users with sign-up, MFA and social login, returning a token. An identity pool handles authorisation: it swaps that token for temporary AWS credentials."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What does STS do, and how long do its credentials last?",a:"It issues temporary credentials when a role is assumed. A role session lasts 1 hour by default and up to 12 hours."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"How do you let account A access a resource in account B?",a:"Create a role in account B that trusts account A, and let account A's identities assume it. No credentials are shared."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What is the IAM credential report?",a:"An account-wide CSV listing every user, their key age, last use and MFA status."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What does IAM Access Advisor show?",a:"For one identity, which services it actually used and when, so you can trim its policies."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"What does IAM Access Analyzer do?",a:"Finds resources shared with external principals, validates policies, and generates least-privilege policies from CloudTrail activity."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"An application on EC2 needs to write to DynamoDB. Should it use access keys in a config file or a role?",a:"A role, attached through an instance profile. Access keys in code, config files or AMIs are the classic exam anti-pattern."},
{d:2,t:"IAM",q:"How many access keys can an IAM user have at once, and why?",a:"Two, so you can rotate to a new key with no downtime."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service detects unusual API calls, compromised credentials or crypto mining, with no agent to install?",a:"Amazon GuardDuty. It reads CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs and DNS logs."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service scans EC2 instances, container images and Lambda functions for known vulnerabilities?",a:"Amazon Inspector."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service finds personal data or credentials sitting in S3?",a:"Amazon Macie."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service investigates the root cause of a GuardDuty finding?",a:"Amazon Detective."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, Security Hub and Detective: what is each one for, in one word?",a:"GuardDuty: threats. Inspector: vulnerabilities. Macie: sensitive data in S3. Security Hub: all findings in one place. Detective: why it happened."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service blocks SQL injection, cross-site scripting, specific countries or too many requests from one IP?",a:"AWS WAF, at layer 7. It attaches to CloudFront, ALB, API Gateway, AppSync and Cognito."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"What is the difference between Shield Standard and Shield Advanced?",a:"Standard is free and automatic on every account, covering layer 3 and 4 DDoS. Advanced is paid and adds a 24/7 DDoS Response Team, cost protection against attack-driven scaling charges, and WAF for protected resources."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service manages WAF, Shield Advanced and security group rules centrally across all accounts?",a:"AWS Firewall Manager."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service is a managed stateful firewall for a whole VPC, with intrusion prevention?",a:"AWS Network Firewall."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service issues and renews TLS certificates for load balancers and CloudFront?",a:"AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which feature shows which IP addresses talked to an instance and whether the traffic was accepted or rejected?",a:"VPC Flow Logs. They record traffic metadata, not the payload."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"What is the difference between Secrets Manager and Systems Manager Parameter Store?",a:"Secrets Manager rotates secrets automatically, is always encrypted with KMS, and is charged per secret. Parameter Store holds configuration values, has no built-in rotation, and its standard tier is free."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service rotates database credentials automatically?",a:"AWS Secrets Manager."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service lets you share a subnet or a Transit Gateway with another account?",a:"AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)."},
{d:2,t:"Security services",q:"Which service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory?",a:"AWS Directory Service."},
{d:2,t:"Network controls",q:"What is the difference between a security group and a network ACL?",a:"A security group works at the instance level, is stateful, and has allow rules only. A network ACL works at the subnet level, is stateless, has allow and deny rules, and evaluates them in number order."},
{d:2,t:"Network controls",q:"You need to block one IP address from reaching a subnet. Security group or network ACL?",a:"Network ACL, with a deny rule. Security groups cannot deny."},
{d:2,t:"Network controls",q:"What do the default security group and the default network ACL allow?",a:"The default security group denies all inbound and allows all outbound. The default NACL allows everything both ways. A custom NACL starts by denying everything."},
{d:2,t:"Network controls",q:"Which firewall allows return traffic automatically?",a:"The security group, because it is stateful."},
{d:2,t:"Trusted Advisor",q:"Which Trusted Advisor checks do the Basic and Developer support plans include?",a:"Only the core checks: S3 bucket permissions, security groups with unrestricted ports, IAM use, MFA on root, public EBS and RDS snapshots, plus all service limit checks."},

// Domain 3: Cloud Technology and Services (34%)
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"When would you use the console, the CLI, an SDK, or infrastructure as code?",a:"Console for exploring and one-off tasks. CLI for scripts. SDK for calling AWS from application code. Infrastructure as code for repeatable, version-controlled environments."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"Which service builds infrastructure from a JSON or YAML template and tracks it as a stack?",a:"AWS CloudFormation."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"Which tool lets you define infrastructure in TypeScript or Python and turns it into CloudFormation?",a:"AWS CDK."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"A developer wants to upload code and have AWS set up the servers, load balancer and scaling, while still being able to see those resources. Which service?",a:"Elastic Beanstalk. The service is free; you pay for the resources it creates."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"Which service turns a container image or source code into a running web service with no infrastructure to manage?",a:"AWS App Runner."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"How do you open a shell on an EC2 instance without opening port 22?",a:"Systems Manager Session Manager."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"What are the Systems Manager capabilities the exam expects you to recognise?",a:"Session Manager, Run Command, Patch Manager, State Manager, Parameter Store, Inventory, Automation and OpsCenter. It works on EC2 and on-premises servers through the SSM Agent."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"What are the three deployment models?",a:"Cloud: everything on AWS. Hybrid: on-premises connected to AWS through Direct Connect, VPN, Outposts or Storage Gateway. On-premises: your own data centre."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"What is the difference between Site-to-Site VPN and Direct Connect?",a:"VPN is an encrypted tunnel over the internet, set up in minutes, cheap, up to 1.25 Gbps per tunnel. Direct Connect is a dedicated private line, weeks to provision, 1 to 100 Gbps, consistent latency, and not encrypted by default."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"Is Direct Connect encrypted?",a:"Not by default. Run a VPN over it or use MACsec if encryption is required."},
{d:3,t:"Deploying",q:"Which service connects individual remote workers to a VPC?",a:"AWS Client VPN."},
{d:3,t:"Global infrastructure",q:"What is a Region, and how many AZs does it have at minimum?",a:"A geographic area, isolated from every other Region, with at least three Availability Zones."},
{d:3,t:"Global infrastructure",q:"What is an Availability Zone?",a:"One or more data centres with their own power, cooling and networking, linked to the other AZs in the Region by low-latency connections."},
{d:3,t:"Global infrastructure",q:"What is an edge location?",a:"A point of presence used by CloudFront and Route 53 to serve content close to users. There are over 600. It is not a Region and runs no instances."},
{d:3,t:"Global infrastructure",q:"A game needs single-digit millisecond latency for players in one city. Which infrastructure option?",a:"A Local Zone: a slice of a Region placed in that metro area."},
{d:3,t:"Global infrastructure",q:"Which option puts AWS compute inside a 5G carrier's network?",a:"AWS Wavelength."},
{d:3,t:"Global infrastructure",q:"Which option puts AWS hardware racks in your own data centre with the same APIs?",a:"AWS Outposts."},
{d:3,t:"Global infrastructure",q:"Which services are global rather than Regional?",a:"IAM, Route 53, CloudFront, Organizations and Artifact. S3 bucket names are global but the buckets themselves are Regional."},
{d:3,t:"Global infrastructure",q:"What is the difference between CloudFront and Global Accelerator?",a:"CloudFront is a CDN that caches HTTP content at edge locations. Global Accelerator gives you two static anycast IPs and routes TCP or UDP traffic over the AWS backbone to the nearest healthy endpoint, with no caching. Use it for gaming, VoIP or anything needing a static IP."},
{d:3,t:"Global infrastructure",q:"Which service provides DNS, domain registration and health-checked routing?",a:"Amazon Route 53."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"What do the EC2 family letters T, M, C, R, I, P and G mean?",a:"T and M are general purpose (T is burstable). C is compute optimised. R is memory optimised. I is storage optimised. P and G are GPU instances."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"What does the instance name m5.large tell you, and what does the g in m7g mean?",a:"Family m, generation 5, size large. The g suffix means a Graviton (ARM) processor."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"What is an AMI, and can you use it in any Region?",a:"A template for the root volume plus launch permissions. It is Regional; copy it to use it elsewhere."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"What is EC2 user data?",a:"A script that runs on the instance's first boot."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"What is the difference between EC2 Auto Scaling and Elastic Load Balancing?",a:"Auto Scaling adds and removes instances to match demand and replaces unhealthy ones. Load balancing spreads traffic across healthy instances in several AZs. One is about capacity, the other about distribution."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"What are the EC2 Auto Scaling policy types?",a:"Manual, scheduled, dynamic (target tracking, step and simple), and predictive."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"What is the difference between an Application, Network and Gateway Load Balancer?",a:"ALB is layer 7 for HTTP and HTTPS, routing by path or host. NLB is layer 4 for TCP and UDP, with a static IP per AZ and very high throughput. GWLB is layer 3 and passes traffic through third-party appliances such as firewalls."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"A third-party firewall appliance must inspect all traffic inline. Which load balancer?",a:"Gateway Load Balancer."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"How long can a Lambda function run, how much memory can it have, and how is it billed?",a:"Up to 15 minutes and up to 10 GB of memory. Billed per request plus duration in milliseconds times memory."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"What is the difference between ECS, EKS and Fargate?",a:"ECS is AWS's own container orchestrator. EKS is managed Kubernetes, portable to other clouds. Fargate runs containers for either without any EC2 instances to manage."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"Where do you store Docker images on AWS?",a:"Amazon ECR."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"A beginner wants a simple WordPress site at a fixed monthly price. Which service?",a:"Amazon Lightsail."},
{d:3,t:"Compute",q:"Which service runs hundreds of thousands of batch jobs and provisions the compute for them?",a:"AWS Batch."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which database engines does RDS support?",a:"MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Oracle, SQL Server and Db2."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"What should you know about Aurora for the exam?",a:"It is AWS-built and compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL, up to five times MySQL throughput, keeps six copies across three AZs, supports up to 15 read replicas, scales storage automatically, and has a Serverless option."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which database is serverless NoSQL with single-digit millisecond latency at any scale?",a:"Amazon DynamoDB. DAX adds a cache; Global Tables add multi-Region."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which service is a petabyte-scale data warehouse for analytics?",a:"Amazon Redshift."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which service is an in-memory cache with sub-millisecond latency, often used for sessions?",a:"Amazon ElastiCache, running Redis or Memcached."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which service is Redis as a durable primary database?",a:"Amazon MemoryDB."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which AWS database is MongoDB-compatible?",a:"Amazon DocumentDB."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which AWS database is Cassandra-compatible?",a:"Amazon Keyspaces."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which AWS database is for graphs, such as social networks or fraud rings?",a:"Amazon Neptune."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which AWS database is for time-series data such as IoT sensor readings?",a:"Amazon Timestream."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"What is the difference between QLDB and Managed Blockchain?",a:"QLDB is an immutable, verifiable ledger owned by one party. Managed Blockchain is for several parties with no central owner, using Hyperledger Fabric or Ethereum."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"What is the difference between RDS Multi-AZ and a read replica?",a:"Multi-AZ is for availability: a synchronous standby with automatic failover, in one Region, serving no reads. A read replica is for scaling reads: an asynchronous copy that can be in another Region."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Does the standby in RDS Multi-AZ serve read traffic?",a:"No. It sits idle until failover. Use read replicas to offload reads."},
{d:3,t:"Databases",q:"Which tool converts an Oracle schema and stored procedures for Aurora PostgreSQL?",a:"AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT). DMS then moves the data."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"What makes a subnet public?",a:"A route in its route table to an Internet Gateway."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"Instances in a private subnet need to download updates from the internet. What do you add?",a:"A NAT Gateway. It allows outbound traffic only, is managed by AWS, and lives in one AZ."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"What is the difference between VPC peering and Transit Gateway?",a:"Peering is a private one-to-one link between two VPCs; it is not transitive and the CIDRs cannot overlap. Transit Gateway is a hub that connects hundreds of VPCs and on-premises networks, and it is transitive."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"Instances in a private subnet need to reach S3 without a NAT Gateway and at no extra cost. What do you add?",a:"A Gateway VPC endpoint. It is free, works through the route table, and supports only S3 and DynamoDB."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"Which feature gives private access to an AWS service or a partner's service through a network interface, without touching the internet?",a:"AWS PrivateLink, through an interface endpoint."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"Which service exposes a Lambda function as a REST API with throttling and API keys?",a:"Amazon API Gateway."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"What are the Route 53 routing policies?",a:"Simple, Weighted, Latency, Failover, Geolocation, Geoproximity, Multivalue answer and IP-based."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"You want to send 10% of traffic to a new version. Which Route 53 policy?",a:"Weighted."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"You want each user sent to the Region that responds fastest for them. Which Route 53 policy?",a:"Latency."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"You want a primary site with a health check and a backup site if it fails. Which Route 53 policy?",a:"Failover."},
{d:3,t:"Networking",q:"You want users routed by the country they are in. Which Route 53 policy?",a:"Geolocation. Geoproximity routes by distance instead, with a bias you can adjust."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"What are the S3 storage classes?",a:"Standard, Intelligent-Tiering, Standard-IA, One Zone-IA, Glacier Instant Retrieval, Glacier Flexible Retrieval and Glacier Deep Archive."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 class should you pick when you do not know the access pattern?",a:"Intelligent-Tiering. It moves objects between tiers for a small monitoring fee and no retrieval charge."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 class is for data accessed rarely but needed instantly, stored in several AZs?",a:"Standard-IA. Thirty-day minimum and a per-GB retrieval fee."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 class is for rarely accessed data that you could recreate if an AZ was lost?",a:"One Zone-IA."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 class is for an archive accessed about once a quarter that still needs millisecond retrieval?",a:"Glacier Instant Retrieval. Ninety-day minimum."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 class is for an archive where waiting minutes to hours for retrieval is fine?",a:"Glacier Flexible Retrieval. Expedited takes 1 to 5 minutes, Standard 3 to 5 hours, Bulk 5 to 12 hours. Ninety-day minimum."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 class is cheapest for a seven-year compliance archive?",a:"Glacier Deep Archive. Retrieval takes 12 to 48 hours. 180-day minimum."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"How durable is S3, and how big can one object be?",a:"Eleven nines of durability for every class; only availability differs. Objects go up to 5 TB. Multipart upload is recommended over 100 MB and required over 5 GB."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 feature moves objects to cheaper classes on a schedule?",a:"Lifecycle rules."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 feature keeps every version of an object and protects against accidental deletes?",a:"Versioning. Once on, it can only be suspended, not disabled."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 feature copies objects to a bucket in another Region?",a:"Cross-Region Replication. Both buckets need versioning on."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 feature makes objects write-once-read-many with retention periods and legal holds?",a:"Object Lock, in Governance or Compliance mode."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which S3 feature speeds up uploads from far away by routing through the nearest edge location?",a:"Transfer Acceleration."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"How do you give someone time-limited access to one private S3 object?",a:"A presigned URL."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"What is EBS, and what are its limits?",a:"A network block volume for a single instance in a single AZ. To move it, snapshot it to S3. It survives a stop; it survives a terminate only if you configure that."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which EBS volume type is the default, which is for high-IOPS databases, and which two are HDD?",a:"gp3 (and gp2) general purpose SSD is the default. io2 and io1 are provisioned IOPS for databases. st1 is throughput HDD for big sequential data; sc1 is cold HDD, the cheapest."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which storage is a fast temporary disk on the host that loses its data when the instance stops?",a:"The EC2 instance store."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Hundreds of Linux instances across AZs need to mount the same file system. EBS or EFS?",a:"EFS. It is managed NFS and scales with use. EBS attaches to one instance in one AZ."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which FSx variant is for Windows SMB shares, and which for high-performance computing?",a:"FSx for Windows File Server for SMB with Active Directory. FSx for Lustre for HPC and ML, integrated with S3."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"What are the three Storage Gateway types?",a:"File Gateway: on-premises NFS or SMB backed by S3. Volume Gateway: iSCSI block storage with EBS snapshots. Tape Gateway: a virtual tape library backed by Glacier."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which service sets backup policies centrally across EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, EFS and S3?",a:"AWS Backup."},
{d:3,t:"Storage",q:"Which service continuously replicates servers so they can be recovered in minutes after a disaster?",a:"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS)."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service lets data scientists build, train and deploy their own ML models?",a:"Amazon SageMaker."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service gives you foundation models for generative AI through an API?",a:"Amazon Bedrock."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service analyses images and video, including faces and content moderation?",a:"Amazon Rekognition."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service extracts text, forms and tables from scanned documents?",a:"Amazon Textract."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service finds sentiment, entities and personal data in text?",a:"Amazon Comprehend."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service builds chatbots with voice and text?",a:"Amazon Lex."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service turns text into speech, and which turns speech into text?",a:"Polly turns text into speech. Transcribe turns speech into text."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service translates text between languages?",a:"Amazon Translate."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service lets employees search internal documents in natural language?",a:"Amazon Kendra."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service gives product recommendations, and which does demand forecasting?",a:"Personalize for recommendations. Forecast for time-series forecasting."},
{d:3,t:"AI/ML",q:"Which service scores transactions for fraud, and which adds human review to ML predictions?",a:"Fraud Detector for fraud scoring. Augmented AI (A2I) for human review."},
{d:3,t:"Analytics",q:"Which service runs SQL queries directly against files in S3 with no servers?",a:"Amazon Athena. You pay per data scanned."},
{d:3,t:"Analytics",q:"Which service runs managed Hadoop and Spark clusters?",a:"Amazon EMR."},
{d:3,t:"Analytics",q:"Which service does serverless ETL and keeps a data catalog?",a:"AWS Glue. Glue DataBrew is the no-code cleaning tool."},
{d:3,t:"Analytics",q:"What is the difference between Kinesis Data Streams and Kinesis Data Firehose?",a:"Data Streams ingests real-time data for your own consumers to process, with retention up to a year. Firehose delivers streams straight into S3, Redshift or OpenSearch with no code, in near real time."},
{d:3,t:"Analytics",q:"Which service is managed Apache Kafka?",a:"Amazon MSK."},
{d:3,t:"Analytics",q:"Which service builds BI dashboards and charges per session?",a:"Amazon QuickSight."},
{d:3,t:"Analytics",q:"Which service does full-text search and log analytics?",a:"Amazon OpenSearch Service."},
{d:3,t:"Analytics",q:"Which service lets you subscribe to third-party datasets?",a:"AWS Data Exchange."},
{d:3,t:"Analytics",q:"Which service builds and secures a data lake on S3?",a:"AWS Lake Formation."},
{d:3,t:"Integration",q:"What is the difference between SQS, SNS, EventBridge and Step Functions?",a:"SQS is a queue that decouples producers from consumers; each message goes to one consumer. SNS is pub/sub that pushes one message to many subscribers. EventBridge is an event bus that routes AWS and SaaS events by rule, and runs schedules. Step Functions orchestrates multi-step workflows as a state machine."},
{d:3,t:"Integration",q:"What is the difference between an SQS standard queue and a FIFO queue?",a:"Standard delivers at least once and may reorder, with unlimited throughput. FIFO delivers exactly once in order, at 300 messages a second (3000 batched)."},
{d:3,t:"Integration",q:"An application already uses ActiveMQ or RabbitMQ. Which service do you migrate it to?",a:"Amazon MQ."},
{d:3,t:"Integration",q:"Which service copies data from Salesforce to S3 with no code?",a:"Amazon AppFlow."},
{d:3,t:"Integration",q:"Which service is a cloud contact centre?",a:"Amazon Connect."},
{d:3,t:"Integration",q:"Which service sends transactional and bulk email?",a:"Amazon SES. Pinpoint is for marketing campaigns across SMS, push and email."},
{d:3,t:"Developer tools",q:"What do CodeBuild, CodeDeploy and CodePipeline each do?",a:"CodeBuild compiles and runs tests. CodeDeploy pushes releases to EC2, Lambda, ECS or on-premises, including blue/green. CodePipeline strings the stages together as CI/CD."},
{d:3,t:"Developer tools",q:"Which service stores npm, pip or Maven packages?",a:"AWS CodeArtifact."},
{d:3,t:"Developer tools",q:"Which service traces one request across microservices to find the slow hop?",a:"AWS X-Ray."},
{d:3,t:"Developer tools",q:"Which service gives you a shell in the browser with the AWS CLI already configured?",a:"AWS CloudShell."},
{d:3,t:"Other services",q:"Which service provides managed virtual desktops?",a:"Amazon WorkSpaces."},
{d:3,t:"Other services",q:"Which service streams a single desktop application to a browser?",a:"Amazon AppStream 2.0."},
{d:3,t:"Other services",q:"Which service builds and hosts full-stack web and mobile apps with auth included?",a:"AWS Amplify."},
{d:3,t:"Other services",q:"Which service provides managed GraphQL APIs?",a:"AWS AppSync."},
{d:3,t:"Other services",q:"Which service tests apps on real phones and browsers?",a:"AWS Device Farm."},
{d:3,t:"Other services",q:"Which service connects IoT devices over MQTT, and which runs code on those devices offline?",a:"IoT Core connects them. IoT Greengrass runs Lambda and ML on the devices at the edge."},
{d:3,t:"Management",q:"Which service holds metrics, alarms, logs and dashboards?",a:"Amazon CloudWatch."},
{d:3,t:"Management",q:"Which service lets teams launch only from a list of approved CloudFormation templates?",a:"AWS Service Catalog."},
{d:3,t:"Management",q:"Which service guides you through sizing and deploying SAP or SQL Server?",a:"AWS Launch Wizard."},
{d:3,t:"Management",q:"Which service assesses an application against its RTO and RPO targets?",a:"AWS Resilience Hub."},
{d:3,t:"Management",q:"Which service injects failures for chaos engineering?",a:"AWS Fault Injection Service."},
{d:3,t:"Management",q:"Which service tells you one of your instances is scheduled for retirement next week?",a:"The AWS Health Dashboard, account view. The service view (formerly the Service Health Dashboard) shows public status for all customers."},

// Domain 4: Billing, Pricing and Support (12%)
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"A workload is short-term and unpredictable and must not be interrupted. Which pricing model?",a:"On-Demand."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"A workload runs all day every day for the next three years. Which pricing model, and how big is the discount?",a:"Reserved Instances, for 1 or 3 years, up to about 72% off On-Demand."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"A batch job can restart from a checkpoint if interrupted. Which pricing model is cheapest?",a:"Spot, up to about 90% off. AWS gives a two-minute warning before reclaiming the instance. Never use Spot for databases or stateful servers."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"A company wants a commitment discount but wants to change instance family or Region freely. Which pricing model?",a:"A Savings Plan. You commit to a dollar amount per hour for 1 or 3 years, not to an instance."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"What is the difference between a Dedicated Host and a Dedicated Instance?",a:"A Dedicated Host is a whole physical server for you, with visibility of sockets and cores, for licences tied to hardware. A Dedicated Instance just runs on hardware no other account shares, with no host-level control, for a per-instance premium."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"How do you guarantee capacity in an AZ for an event without a long-term commitment?",a:"An On-Demand Capacity Reservation. Any length, billed at the On-Demand rate."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"What is the difference between a Standard and a Convertible Reserved Instance?",a:"Standard has the bigger discount, cannot change family or OS, and can be sold on the RI Marketplace. Convertible has a smaller discount, can be exchanged for a different type of equal or greater value, and cannot be sold."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"What are the Reserved Instance payment options, and which gives the biggest discount?",a:"All Upfront, Partial Upfront and No Upfront. All Upfront for three years is the biggest discount."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"What is the difference between a Regional and a Zonal Reserved Instance?",a:"Regional applies the discount across any AZ but reserves no capacity. Zonal reserves capacity in one specific AZ."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"What is the difference between a Compute Savings Plan and an EC2 Instance Savings Plan?",a:"Compute covers EC2, Fargate and Lambda in any family, size, OS or Region, with a lower discount. EC2 Instance covers EC2 only, locked to one family in one Region, with a higher discount."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"Which Savings Plan also covers Lambda and Fargate?",a:"The Compute Savings Plan."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"What happens to usage above your Savings Plan commitment?",a:"It is billed at the On-Demand rate."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"Where can you sell a Reserved Instance you no longer need?",a:"The Reserved Instance Marketplace. Standard RIs only, and you must register as a seller using the root user."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"Do Reserved Instance discounts apply across accounts?",a:"Yes, in an Organization with consolidated billing. Sharing is on by default and can be turned off per account."},
{d:4,t:"Pricing models",q:"How is EC2 time billed?",a:"Per second with a 60-second minimum for Linux, Windows and other OS-flexible instances. RHEL and SUSE are billed per hour."},
{d:4,t:"Data transfer",q:"Which data transfer is free and which is billed?",a:"Free: internet into AWS, same-AZ traffic over private IPs, S3 or an origin out to CloudFront. Billed: out to the internet (tiered), between AZs in both directions, between Regions, and same-AZ traffic over public or Elastic IPs."},
{d:4,t:"Data transfer",q:"Two instances in the same AZ talk over private IP addresses. Is that traffic billed?",a:"No, that is free."},
{d:4,t:"Data transfer",q:"Two instances in different AZs of the same Region talk to each other. Is that traffic billed?",a:"Yes, in both directions."},
{d:4,t:"Data transfer",q:"Is data from S3 out to CloudFront billed?",a:"No. Nor is S3 to a Gateway endpoint in the same Region."},
{d:4,t:"Storage pricing",q:"What does S3 charge you for?",a:"GB stored per month, requests per thousand, retrieval per GB on IA and Glacier classes, a minimum storage duration, data transfer out, and early deletion."},
{d:4,t:"Storage pricing",q:"What are the minimum storage durations for Standard-IA, Glacier Flexible Retrieval and Deep Archive?",a:"30 days, 90 days and 180 days. Deleting early is charged for the rest of the period."},
{d:4,t:"Storage pricing",q:"How is EBS billed?",a:"On provisioned GB per month whether you write to it or not, plus provisioned IOPS and throughput above baseline on io and gp3 volumes. Snapshots are billed on changed blocks only."},
{d:4,t:"Free Tier",q:"What are the three types of Free Tier offer?",a:"Always free, such as 1 million Lambda requests a month or 25 GB of DynamoDB. Twelve months free from account creation, such as 750 hours of t3.micro or 5 GB of S3. Short trials that start when you first use a service."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"Which tool alerts you when spend passes a threshold, and can stop instances automatically when it does?",a:"AWS Budgets. Budget Actions can apply an IAM or SCP policy or stop EC2 and RDS instances."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"Which tool shows spending trends, which service costs the most, and a forecast?",a:"Cost Explorer. It looks 13 months back and forecasts 12 months ahead, and recommends RIs, Savings Plans and right-sizing."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"Which tool gives the most detailed billing data, and where does it land?",a:"The Cost and Usage Report (CUR), delivered to an S3 bucket with hourly line items. Query it with Athena."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"Which tool estimates the cost of an architecture before you build it, with no AWS account needed?",a:"AWS Pricing Calculator."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"When do you use the Pricing Calculator, Cost Explorer and the Cost and Usage Report?",a:"Pricing Calculator before building, for an estimate. Cost Explorer while running, for daily or monthly analysis and forecasts. CUR while running, for hourly line-item detail in S3."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"How do you break the bill down by project or team?",a:"Cost allocation tags. AWS-generated ones start with aws:, user-defined ones with user:. They must be activated in the Billing console before they show up in Cost Explorer or the CUR."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"A tag was added to every EC2 instance but does not appear in Cost Explorer. Why?",a:"It has not been activated as a cost allocation tag in the Billing console."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"Which tool groups accounts and tags into named business units by rule?",a:"Cost Categories."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"Which tool shows business units a bill with custom rates, markups or credits?",a:"AWS Billing Conductor."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"Which tool alerts you to unusual spend without you setting a threshold?",a:"Cost Anomaly Detection. It uses machine learning."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"Which tool uses machine learning to recommend a smaller instance based on actual utilisation?",a:"AWS Compute Optimizer. It covers EC2, Auto Scaling groups, EBS, Lambda and ECS on Fargate."},
{d:4,t:"Cost tools",q:"What is the difference between Compute Optimizer, Trusted Advisor and Cost Explorer for right-sizing?",a:"Compute Optimizer uses ML on CloudWatch metrics to size resources. Trusted Advisor runs broad checks such as idle load balancers and underused instances. Cost Explorer shows right-sizing alongside spend analysis and RI recommendations."},
{d:4,t:"Organizations",q:"What does consolidated billing give you?",a:"One bill for every account, paid by the management account. Volume discount tiers are reached on combined usage. RI and Savings Plan discounts are shared by default. Each member still sees its own usage."},
{d:4,t:"Organizations",q:"Can you use consolidated billing without the rest of Organizations?",a:"Yes. Consolidated billing works on its own. SCPs need all-features mode."},
{d:4,t:"Organizations",q:"What is AWS Marketplace, and how is it billed?",a:"A catalogue of third-party software and SaaS, charged on your AWS invoice. Options include hourly, annual, BYOL, SaaS subscriptions and private offers. Private Marketplace restricts what users can buy."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"What are the five Support plans?",a:"Basic, Developer, Business, Enterprise On-Ramp and Enterprise."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"What does the Basic plan include?",a:"It is free. Account and billing help only, no technical cases, core Trusted Advisor checks, documentation and re:Post."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"What does the Developer plan include?",a:"One primary contact can open cases, by email, in business hours. General guidance in 24 hours, system impaired in 12 hours. General architectural guidance."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"What does the Business plan add over Developer?",a:"Unlimited contacts, 24x7 phone, chat and email, production down answered in 1 hour, production impaired in 4 hours, the full Trusted Advisor set, third-party software guidance, the Support API, and architectural guidance for your use case."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"What does Enterprise On-Ramp add over Business?",a:"Business-critical down answered in 30 minutes, a pool of Technical Account Managers rather than a named one, the Concierge team, Infrastructure Event Management, and consultative reviews."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"What does the Enterprise plan add over Enterprise On-Ramp?",a:"Business-critical down answered in 15 minutes, a designated Technical Account Manager, and Well-Architected reviews."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"Which is the cheapest plan that answers a production system down case within an hour?",a:"Business."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"How fast is a business-critical system down case answered on Enterprise, and on Enterprise On-Ramp?",a:"15 minutes on Enterprise. 30 minutes on Enterprise On-Ramp."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"What are the response times for general guidance, system impaired, production impaired and production down?",a:"General guidance 24 hours and system impaired 12 hours from Developer up. Production impaired 4 hours and production down 1 hour from Business up."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"Which is the cheapest plan with 24x7 phone support and the full Trusted Advisor checks?",a:"Business."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"Who can change the support plan?",a:"Only the root user."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"What does the Concierge Support Team handle?",a:"Billing and account questions for Enterprise customers. It is not technical support."},
{d:4,t:"Support plans",q:"Which plans include the AWS Health API and the Support API?",a:"Business and above."},
{d:4,t:"Trusted Advisor",q:"What are the Trusted Advisor check categories?",a:"Cost optimisation, performance, security, fault tolerance, service limits and operational excellence."},
{d:4,t:"Trusted Advisor",q:"Which service flags idle load balancers, unattached Elastic IPs and quotas above 80% usage?",a:"Trusted Advisor."},
{d:4,t:"Trusted Advisor",q:"Which support plans get the full Trusted Advisor check set?",a:"Business, Enterprise On-Ramp and Enterprise. Basic and Developer get only the core security checks and service limits."},
{d:4,t:"Support resources",q:"Where do you open a support case, and where do you ask the community?",a:"Support Center for cases. re:Post for the community; it replaced the AWS Forums and is free. The Knowledge Center has self-service articles."},
{d:4,t:"Support resources",q:"Which service lets you hire a certified AWS expert for a few hours?",a:"AWS IQ."},
{d:4,t:"Support resources",q:"Which option gives you AWS's own consultants for a large migration?",a:"AWS Professional Services."},
{d:4,t:"Support resources",q:"Which option has AWS run your infrastructure day to day?",a:"AWS Managed Services (AMS)."},
{d:4,t:"Support resources",q:"What is a Technical Account Manager?",a:"A named contact inside AWS who gives proactive, ongoing guidance. Enterprise plans only; On-Ramp gets a shared pool."},
{d:4,t:"Support resources",q:"What are the AWS Partner Network partner types?",a:"Consulting partners do migrations and managed services. Technology partners sell software on AWS, usually via Marketplace. Training partners deliver courses. Distribution partners resell AWS. Tiers are Select, Advanced and Premier."},
{d:4,t:"Support resources",q:"Which programme gives startups credits, support and training?",a:"AWS Activate."}
];
